  "query_preview": "(This Evening through Monday)... \nIssued at 220 PM EDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nMuch cooler air has overspread the region in the wake of the \nfrontal passage late Sunday. Cloudy and breezy conditions are \nexpected through Wednesday as an upper low moves slowly through the \narea. This feature will cutoff over the mid Atlantic by late \nweek...with much drier air being pulled south into the Ohio Valley \nthrough the weekend.\n\nRest of Today through Wednesday Night\n\nIn the wake of the morning rain...a damp and dreary airmass remains \nacross central Indiana under a thick stratus deck and with breezy \nnortheast winds. 18Z temperatures were much cooler than what has \nbeen experienced over the last several days with much of central \nIndiana in the 60s.\n\nThere remain a few light showers or sprinkles over the southeast \nhalf of the forecast area but the overall trend since the morning \nhas been for rainfall coverage to decrease extensively as the \nfrontal boundary that moved through the area Sunday afternoon and \nevening has now moved well south into the Tennessee Valley.A few \nlight showers remain possible for the next few hours before \ndeparting. Abundant moisture persists through the boundary layer \ntrapped beneath a stout inversion. Model soundings show the \ninversion weakening slightly this evening which may be just enough \nto allow for a brief period with breaks in the stratus deck. As the \ninversion strengthens overnight however...expect the stratus deck to \nthicken back up with ceilings lowering again into Tuesday morning. \nCannot entirely rule out a few sprinkles tonight but low confidence \nin this occurring.\n\nClouds will remain on Tuesday as an upper low pivots into the region \nfrom the northwest. The upper low will bring isolated light showers \nacross the far northeast portion of the forecast area otherwise \nexpect a continuation of weather today with thick stratus and cool \nN/NE flow. The upper low will be slow to shift east of the area as \nit becomes cutoff from the mean flow aloft well to the north of the \nregion. This will keep an abundance of clouds over most of the \nforecast area through Wednesday as well with again a low chance for \na few showers across our northeast counties. More substantial breaks \nin the clouds should come Wednesday night as ridging aloft slowly \nspreads into the Ohio Valley from the west.\n\nTemps...highs will not get out of the 60s this afternoon for much of \nthe forecast area...the coolest day since May 23. Expect similar \ntemperatures both Tuesday and Wednesday with highs ranging from the \nmid 60s northeast to the lower 70s southwest. Lows will be in the \n50s.\n\nThursday through Sunday\n\nThe aforementioned upper low will settle over the Mid Atlantic \nregion during the second half of the week. Cool E/NE flow and at \nleast some lingering cloud cover on the back side of the developing \ncutoff low will maintain below normal temperatures Thursday with \nhighs ranging from the upper 60s to the mid 70s from northeast to \nsouthwest. Ridging aloft will gradually shift into the Ohio Valley \nwith an axis of deepening subsidence advecting into the region from \nthe north. Under mainly clear skies...temperatures will recover back \nto late September normals with highs warming into the mid and upper \n70s by Sunday and perhaps into the lower 80s over parts of the \nforecast area by Monday. The coolest night since the Spring comes \nFriday night as lows in the mid and upper 40s will be common...with \ntemperatures recovering into the 50s for lows by the end of the \nweekend.",
